-- Full model answers for the eleven course Writing tasks. A sample has one paragraph per part of the essay in
-- order (Introduction, Overview, Body 1, Body 2 for Task 1; Introduction, Body 1, Body 2, Conclusion for Task 2),
-- separated by a blank line, and marks the phrases worth learning with **double asterisks**. Learners see it only
-- after an essay has been graded; the grader never reads it.

WITH samples (pkg_code, sample) AS (VALUES
('PS-W1-WR-1', $sample$
The bar chart compares the **car ownership rate** among households in four countries, labelled A, B, C and D, in 2000 and in 2020.

Overall, the proportion of households with a car increased in three of the four countries, and Country D was the only one to show a fall, which was slight. The country with the highest figure also changed, because D led in 2000 but A had taken first place by 2020.

Countries A and D had the highest ownership rates at the start of the period. In 2000, D stood at 70%, which was ten percentage points above A at 60%. By 2020, however, A had **recorded a rise** to 72%, whereas D had slipped to 68%, so the two countries finished with **a narrow gap** of only four percentage points.

**By comparison**, B and C began from lower levels but both grew considerably. B rose from 45% to 58%, a gain of 13 percentage points. C showed the biggest change, climbing from 30% to 55%, an increase of 25 points, which narrowed its gap with B from 15 points to just three. Even so, C still had the lowest rate in both years.
$sample$),
('PS-W1-WR-2', $sample$
The two pie charts show how an average family's **budget allocation** between five categories changed between 1990 and 2020.

Overall, the pattern of spending changed noticeably over the thirty years. Housing replaced food as the biggest item, and while the shares of income spent on housing, transport and leisure all grew, those for food and other expenses declined, with the fall in food being the most significant change.

In 1990, food was the main cost and managed to **take the largest share** of income at 30%, followed by housing at 25%. By 2020, housing had become the leading category after **a ten-point rise** to 35%, whereas food had dropped by twelve points to 18%, which was the largest fall of any category.

The remaining categories showed smaller changes. Transport increased from 10% to 15% and leisure from 15% to 20%, so each gained five percentage points. By contrast, other spending fell from 20% to 12%, which meant that it represented **a smaller proportion** of the budget at the end of the period. Together, these changes suggest that over the period families devoted more of their income to housing, travel and free time, and less to food and miscellaneous items.
$sample$),
('PS-W1-WR-3', $sample$
The table gives information about the number of religious and civil marriages and the average age at which brides and grooms married in Australia between 1960 and 2000.

Overall, both types of marriage became more common over the forty years, and people tended to marry later. Religious marriages were more frequent than civil ones for most of the period, but civil marriages overtook them in 2000, while grooms were always older than brides.

In 1960, religious marriages were far more common than civil ones, with **respective figures** of 25,000 and 8,000. The religious figure then **doubled and peaked at** 50,000 in 1980, before falling to 32,000 in 1990 and recovering slightly to 34,000 in 2000. Meanwhile, civil marriages rose more steadily, from 12,000 in 1970 to 16,000 in 1980 and 23,000 in 1990, followed by **a sharp rise** to 40,000 in 2000, at which point they had **overtaken** religious ones.

Turning to age, brides were consistently younger than their male **counterparts**. In 1960, the average bride was 21 and the average groom 24, a gap of three years. Both figures then **rose steadily**, by one to three years each decade, and by 2000 they had reached 30 and 32 respectively. This means that both groups were marrying roughly eight or nine years later than in 1960.
$sample$),
('PS-W5-WR-1', $sample$
The line graph shows the **monthly attendance** at a national park over one year, with the figures given in thousands.

Overall, visitor numbers followed a clear seasonal pattern. They rose steadily from the start of the year to a peak in the summer, then fell sharply during the autumn before recovering slightly at the very end of the year. In other words, the park was busiest in the middle of the year and quietest in the winter months.

In January, the park received only 20 thousand visitors, and the figure edged up to 22 thousand in February. From March, the numbers climbed quickly, reaching 35 thousand in March, 50 thousand in April and 70 thousand in May. This upward trend continued until the park **reached a high point** of 110 thousand visitors in July, after 90 thousand in June.

After the peak, there was **a marked decline**. The figure dipped slightly to 105 thousand in August and then dropped to 60 thousand in September, followed by 40 thousand in October and 25 thousand in November. December saw **a modest recovery** to 30 thousand, which was still ten thousand higher than the figure for **the opening month** of the year.
$sample$),
('PS-W5-WR-2', $sample$
The line graph compares the average **pump price** of a litre of petrol and of diesel, in dollars, between 2015 and 2020.

Overall, the two fuels followed very similar patterns: both became cheaper in 2016, rose to a peak in 2018 and then declined. In addition, diesel was cheaper than petrol until 2017 but more expensive from 2018 onwards.

In 2015, petrol cost $1.10 per litre and diesel $1.00, but both prices fell in 2016, to $1.05 and $0.95 respectively. They then climbed to $1.20 and $1.15 in 2017, and the two series **reached a joint peak** in 2018, when petrol stood at $1.35 and diesel at $1.40.

After that, prices declined, slowly at first and then more sharply. Petrol dropped to $1.30 in 2019 and diesel to $1.38, and by 2020 they had decreased further to $1.00 and $1.05. Diesel therefore **finished above its baseline** of $1.00, whereas petrol ended ten cents below its opening price. The movement also showed **a reversal in order**, since diesel had been the cheaper fuel at the start of the period but became the more expensive one from 2018.
$sample$),
('PS-W2-WR-1', $sample$
Some people argue that governments should pay university fees for every student. While publicly funded tuition could **widen participation** in higher education, I believe that financial support should be directed at students who could not otherwise afford a degree, rather than offered to everyone.

The strongest argument for removing fees is that cost is a serious **financial barrier** for many talented young people. When tuition is cheaper, a student from a low-income household can choose a course according to ability instead of affordability. For example, a school-leaver with the grades to study engineering might abandon the idea because of the debt involved, but with help towards fees that student could enrol, and society would gain another skilled graduate. In this way, public support for universities can make opportunities more equal. Equal access of this kind also benefits employers, who gain a larger pool of qualified workers.

However, making university free for all would take a large share of the national budget, and it would not solve every problem. Governments must **allocate public funds** between education, healthcare and many other services, and students still face **living expenses** such as rent and food, which free tuition does not cover. A more efficient approach is **targeted assistance**, for instance scholarships based on family income, maintenance grants and loans that are repaid only when graduates earn enough. Money would then reach the students who need it most, while families that can afford fees would continue to contribute. This is also fairer to taxpayers who never attend university.

In conclusion, although free tuition would improve access to higher education, I believe that means-tested support is fairer and more realistic. It removes the barrier for those in need without placing an excessive burden on public funding.
$sample$),
('PS-W2-WR-2', $sample$
Many people think that children should start learning a foreign language in primary school rather than in secondary school. I agree with an earlier start, provided that lessons are **age-appropriate** and taught by trained teachers.

The main advantage of beginning early is the extra **language exposure** that young learners receive. Hearing and using simple expressions regularly over several years can build **a solid foundation** for later study, because children are usually willing to imitate sounds and are less afraid of making mistakes. For instance, pupils who greet one another, sing songs and play games in English before they meet complex grammar gradually become familiar with common phrases. Early lessons also make learning feel natural, since children treat a new language as part of play rather than as a subject to be tested. As a result, when they reach secondary school, they can concentrate on more advanced skills instead of starting from zero.

Nevertheless, an early start only works if the programme is well designed. A heavy timetable might take **curriculum time** away from core subjects, and untrained teachers could introduce errors that are hard to correct later. These risks can be reduced with short, enjoyable lessons, a **trained instructor** for every class and a **gradual progression** from simple to more difficult material. Learners who begin in secondary school may understand grammar rules more quickly, but they have fewer years of contact with the language at school. Therefore, the decisive factor is not only the age of starting but also the quality of the course.

In conclusion, I agree that foreign-language teaching should begin in primary school, as long as the lessons suit children's needs. With careful planning, pupils will gain longer exposure and better results.
$sample$),
('PS-W3-WR-1', $sample$
In some cities, public transport is free for everyone. In my opinion, the advantages of this policy can outweigh the disadvantages, provided that enough funding is available to keep services reliable.

The main drawback of **fare-free travel** is the financial burden of running the network. Removing tickets does not remove **operating costs**, because vehicles still need maintenance and drivers still need to be paid. For example, if a city cannot replace the lost income, it may reduce **service frequency**, and passengers would then wait much longer at stops. In practice, even a small reduction in the number of buses can undo the benefits of free tickets. Such **a funding shortfall** would damage the quality of the service and discourage people from using it. This problem is serious, but it can be managed if the money is raised through local taxes or road charges.

Nevertheless, better **access to essential journeys** can make the policy worthwhile. Residents on low incomes often spend a large part of their wages on travel, so free buses and trains help them to reach work, school and hospitals. Moreover, drivers are more likely to leave their cars at home if public transport is convenient and dependable. For instance, a commuter who no longer pays a fare and can rely on frequent trains may decide to stop driving, which would reduce traffic and pollution. Free transport can also encourage visitors to explore a city, which supports local shops and restaurants. The benefits to the whole community are therefore greater than the cost of the lost fares.

In conclusion, free public transport has real costs, but I believe that its advantages are greater, as long as cities fund it properly and maintain frequent services for the people who depend on them.
$sample$),
('PS-W3-WR-2', $sample$
Some people say that working from home is better for employees than working in an office, while others disagree. Although both views have merit, I favour **a flexible arrangement** that allows remote work while preserving time for face-to-face collaboration.

Supporters of home working value the flexibility and the reduced **commuting time** it offers. Employees who **work remotely** save hours each week that would otherwise be spent travelling, and a quiet home can support focused tasks. For example, someone writing a long report can plan blocks of uninterrupted time, which is difficult in a busy open-plan office. Many employees also say that they can balance family responsibilities more easily when they are not travelling to work every day. These benefits depend on the type of job and on having a suitable space at home, however.

Others argue that **a shared workplace** makes communication and practical teamwork easier. New staff often need guidance from colleagues, quick questions are answered faster in person, and some jobs require equipment that is only available on site. For instance, a team testing a new product can solve problems more efficiently when its members are in the same room, because they can see the results together and agree on changes at once. Working alone, on the other hand, can leave some employees feeling disconnected from their colleagues and from the goals of the organisation. Since different tasks need different settings, neither option suits every job, and a mixed schedule gives the best of both: concentration at home and **face-to-face contact** at work.

In conclusion, home working and office working each have clear strengths, and I believe that a **task-dependent**, flexible approach is better than choosing only one of them. Employers should therefore let people use the setting that best suits each task.
$sample$),
('PS-W4-WR-1', $sample$
**Online retail** has increasingly replaced visits to traditional shops in many countries. This trend has several causes, and it is changing both the role of physical stores and what customers expect from them.

Convenience is a major reason why consumers increasingly choose to buy on the internet. Because websites are open at all hours, people can browse and order in the evening or at weekends without travelling anywhere. In addition, shoppers can **compare product ranges** and prices from many sellers within minutes, which is difficult to do on foot. Lower prices are another cause, since online sellers often have smaller running costs than shops with expensive premises. For instance, a busy parent can order a washing machine late at night and have it delivered to the door, saving both time and effort. Therefore, many people now regard the internet as their first choice for buying goods.

This shift is changing both the role of physical shops and the services that customers expect. Stores that rely only on walk-in trade are losing sales, so many now combine online ordering with **in-store collection** or returns. A clothes shop, for example, can let customers order sizes online and exchange them locally. Some towns have even seen empty shop units appear on their high streets, which shows how deeply the change has affected local economies. On the other hand, buyers face new problems, such as **delivery waiting time** and the inability to try products before paying. As a result, **consumer expectations** are rising, and retailers must offer fast delivery and easy returns if they want to keep their customers.

In conclusion, convenience and choice are the main causes of the growth of online shopping, and its chief effects are that shops must adapt their services while customers expect faster and more flexible treatment.
$sample$),
('PS-W4-WR-2', $sample$
Some people think that governments should spend money on public transport rather than on building new roads. I largely agree that public transport should receive greater priority, although essential road infrastructure still requires investment.

A reliable **transport network** can give more residents an alternative to **private vehicle use**. When buses and trains connect homes with workplaces and services, people who do not own a car can still reach jobs and education. For example, a new bus route linking a residential area with a school and an employment centre would let young people travel safely and cheaply. Public transport also uses road space and fuel more efficiently, because one bus can carry the passengers of dozens of cars. As a result, fewer cars would be on the road, and congestion and pollution would fall, which new roads alone rarely achieve because they tend to attract extra traffic.

However, prioritising public transport does not remove the need for essential roads. Ambulances and fire engines require clear routes, **freight movement** depends on lorries, and **rural coverage** by buses is often poor because few people live in each area. For instance, a village without a regular service may depend entirely on a good road to reach the nearest town. It would be unrealistic to expect a bus network to replace every road, especially where distances are long. Investment should therefore reflect local needs, with new roads built where they serve emergencies or goods, and the larger share of funds going to public services.

In conclusion, I agree with giving public transport greater priority, but with limits. A balanced policy would improve travel for most people while still protecting **emergency access** and the delivery of goods. Governments should therefore plan both systems together instead of treating them as rivals.
$sample$)
)
UPDATE question_versions qv
SET explanation = s.sample
FROM content_packages p
JOIN content_sections cs ON cs.package_version_id = p.current_published_version_id
JOIN section_questions sq ON sq.section_id = cs.id
JOIN samples s ON s.pkg_code = p.code
WHERE qv.id = sq.question_version_id
  AND qv.answer_spec ->> 'type' = 'ESSAY';
